{In the Stevenson Memorial tournament (referred to as the 11th Bognor Regis
International Congress in BCM (SP)) 1963 I drew in the first round, and so
expected a "modest" opponent in round two. I found myself facing Bob Wade IM
and British Champion on more than one occasion. Time limit 45 moves in 2 hr
30' then 18 per hr.} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. e4 {By playing the moves in this
order White avoids the Grunfeld Defence.} d6 4. d4 Bg7 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7.
d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. exd5 {Has the advantage of leaving the White KBishop on
an open diagonal after Bd3 and also it is very difficult for Black to get any
counterplay by ...b5 as White has a Pawn on c4. White holds Black up on the QS.
} ({I had expected.} 9. cxd5) 9... Na6 10. O-O Nc7 11. Bd3 a6 ({The better
plan was to prevent f5 by White by} 11... Bd7 {and 12...Qc8.}) 12. a4 Rb8 ({
Blockading the f-pawn would still be better. Interesting would be} 12... Nh5 {
followed by ...f5.}) 13. a5 Na8 {Much too artificial as White can now get
right on top by 14 f5; however, this move could hardly be prevented.} (13...
Bd7 {still seemed slightly better.}) 14. f5 b6 {Gaining some freedom although
the a-pawn is left rather weak.} 15. axb6 Nxb6 {Hoping to be able to manoeuvre
the Knight to e5.} 16. Bf4 {Prevents ...Nd7, ...Ne5 or ...Bd7} Qc7 {Defending
the d-Pawn and hoping to get the bRook to the e-file.} 17. Ra2 {Defending the
b-Pawn and keeping my a-Pawn attacked.} Bb7 {With this move I hoped to be able
to play .....Rbd8 but I don't get the chance and the bishop has to move back
again.} 18. fxg6 fxg6 19. Ng5 Bc8 20. h3 {Preventing 20.....Bg4 and giving his
Bishop a square at h2.} Nh5 {The move I was so much relying on to get me out
of trouble.} 21. Bh2 Rxf1+ 22. Bxf1 {[58']} Qe7 {[80'] My position seemed to
have suddenly improved; However} 23. Qe2 $1 ({This move seems to win the game.
After} 23. Nge4 {(either Knight) Black would have had a reasonable game.})
23... Be5 {[99'] There doesn't sem anything better than this.} ({After...} 
23... Qxe2 24. Bxe2 {I have weaknesses all over the place and White would
probably win comfortably.}) (23... Qxg5 24. Bxd6 {loses straight off.}) 24.
Bxe5 dxe5 25. Nge4 {Now I have three isolated Pawns and no hope of defending
them all.} Nd7 26. Qe3 Ng7 ({I hoped to be able to get in .....Nf5. Slightly
better chances were offered by} 26... Nhf6 {but White should still win.}) 27.
d6 Qe6 28. Nxc5 Qxd6 29. Nxd7 Bxd7 30. Ne4 {[91'] Forcing the win in neat
style.} Qb6 {[130']} (30... Qc7 31. Nf6+ {wins the e-Pawn} (31. Rxa6)) (30...
Qe7 31. Rxa6) (30... Qd4 31. Qxd4 exd4 32. Nf6+) (30... Qe6 31. Nc5 (31. Qa7))
(30... Qc6 31. Qg5 (31. c5 {wins the e or a Pawn.})) 31. c5 Qe6 32. Rxa6 Bc6 (
32... Qd5 33. Nf6+) 33. Ng5 Qd5 34. Rxc6 $1 Qxc6 35. Bc4+ Kf8 36. Qxe5 {
R.G.Wade came 6-10th with 7pts. Source: Dick Collins scorebook, contributed by
Sean Parker, 19 December 2022.} 1-0

