{Time limit 45 moves in 2 hr 30' then 18 per hr} 1. c4 e5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 d5
4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Nc3 Nb6 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. a3 a5 {A good move preventing
9.b4} 9. d3 O-O 10. Be3 {This doesn't turn out too well. Fianchettoing the
bishop may be better. Black has rather the better of it now.} Be6 {[26']} 11.
Ne4 {Too artificial.} Rb8 {[48'] To defend the b-pawn after 12 Bc5 Bxc5 13
Nxc5.} 12. Qc2 {[18']} (12. Bc5 Bxc5 13. Nxc5) 12... f5 {Now Black gets right
on top.} 13. Nc3 (13. Nc5 {The Knight might become a liability here.}) 13...
Bf6 {[76']} 14. Bc5 {[28']} (14. Bxb6 $142) 14... Rf7 15. Bxb6 cxb6 {The
opening of the c-file is much better for Black.} 16. Nb5 {Good moves are
conspicuous by their absence.} Rc8 {[83']} 17. Qa4 ({Here the queen gets cut
off.} 17. Qd2 e4) 17... Rd7 18. Rab1 {To play Nd2 without ...e5.} Kh8 19. Rfd1
$2 {This loses the exchange but my position was very bad anyway.} Qg8 20. b4 {
[51']} ({If} 20. Rdc1 Bb3 {wins the queen.}) 20... Ba2 21. bxa5 (21. Rb2 e4)
21... Bxb1 22. Rxb1 Ra8 {I thought of resigning but Black was short of time so
I played on.} 23. a6 Rxa6 {Now my a-pawn must fall.} 24. Qc2 Be7 25. Qc3 Qa2
26. Rc1 Bf6 {The next twenty moves by Black were played in time trouble.} 27.
Nd2 {Fishing for what might come up if I can complicate matters.} e4 28. d4 {
This shaky move is by far my best opportunity.} Ra5 29. Rc2 Qg8 30. a4 Rxa4 31.
e3 {Defends the d-pawn and gets the bishop into play.} Ra5 32. Bf1 Qf8 33. Qc4
g6 34. Nb3 Ra8 35. Qe6 Rad8 36. Qc4 Qf7 37. Qc3 {To exchange queens is as good
as resigning.} Qe7 38. Qc4 Kg7 39. Rd2 {To give the Queen a retreat} Qb4 40.
Qc2 Qe7 {Black is happy to mark time until the time control is reached.} 41.
Bc4 {Now d5 is in the air.} Nb4 42. Qd1 Nd5 {Black, it appears, has made all
secure.} 43. g4 {The only hope.} Qb4 $2 {Black would do better to leave the
queen on the KS.} 44. Bxd5 Qxb5 (44... Rxd5 45. Nc7 R5d6 46. d5 {gives White
some chances.}) 45. Be6 Rd6 {[149']} 46. gxf5 gxf5 {[152'] Black now realised
that White had secured all sorts of chances. White threatens 47.d5 followed by
48.Qh5} 47. d5 {Black is now in a quandary. There seems to be no move to
maintain his advantage. White's Bishop at e6 covers all the important squares.
Therefore...} Rxe6 $1 {This appears to be good} 48. Nd4 $1 ({This in between
move secures the draw. After} 48. dxe6 Rxd2 49. Nxd2 {White has a hopeless
endgame, but}) (48. Nd4 $1 Bxd4 49. dxe6 {There is no win, e.g.} Qe5 50. e7 $1)
48... Bxd4 49. dxe6 {[85'] To draw from the position after 22 moves is quite
fantastic. I played the opening far too carelessly. P.M.Collins came = 18th -
23rd with 6pts. Source: Dick Collins scorebook, contributed by Sean Parker, 19
December 2022.} 1/2-1/2

